    @section thread_resources Thread Resources

    %Qore supports a concept called thread resources; thread resources are resources that belong to a particular
    thread and that require some sort of cleanup of the resource in case the thread is exited without releasing the
    resource.  Examples of thread resources in %Qore are as follows:
    - @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex": when a lock is acquired, the lock is tracked as a thread resource.  If the
      thread is exited without releasing the lock, the @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex" thread resource cleanup
      routine is executed which releases the lock and throws a user-friendly exception explaining what happened.  When
      the lock is released, the thread resource is removed.
    - @ref Qore::Thread::RWLock "RWLock": read-write locks are treated as thread resources in the same way described
      for @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex" locks
    - @ref Qore::Thread::Gate "Gate": gate locks are treated as thread resources in the same way described for
      @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex" locks
    - @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource": the transaction lock is handled as a thread resource; if a thread exits
      holding the transaction lock, the @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource "Datasource" cleanup routine is executed; this will
      rollback the transaction (freeing the transaction lock) and throw an appropriate exception.
    - @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool "DatasourcePool": datasource allocations are handled as a thread resources; if a
      thread exits without releasing the connection, the @ref Qore::SQL::DatasourcePool "DatasourcePool" cleanup
      routine is executed; this will rollback the transaction (releasing the dtasource connection back to the pool)
      and throw an appropriate exception.

    User classes can implement their own thread resource handling by subclassing
    @ref Qore::Thread::AbstractThreadResource "AbstractThreadResource"  (implementing custom cleanup code in the
    @ref Qore::Thread::AbstractThreadResource::cleanup() "AbstractThreadResource::cleanup()" method) and then calling:
    - @ref Qore::set_thread_resource() "set_thread_resource()": to set the resource against the thread
    - @ref Qore::remove_thread_resource() "remove_thread_resource()": to remove the resource from the thread

    If the thread is exited or one of the thread resource cleanup functions is called while the thread resource is
    set, then the @ref Qore::Thread::AbstractThreadResource::cleanup() "AbstractThreadResource::cleanup()" method is
    called, which should cleanup the thread resource and normally throw a user-friendly exception describing the
    error, the cleanup actions takenm and how to prevent it in the future.

    The thread-resource cleanup functions are:
    - @ref Qore::throw_thread_resource_exceptions() "throw_thread_resource_exceptions()": runs thread-resource cleanup
      routines explicitly
    - @ref Qore::throw_thread_resource_exceptions_to_mark() "throw_thread_resource_exceptions_to_mark()": runs
      thread-resource cleanup routines for all thread resources set since the last explicit call to
      @ref Qore::mark_thread_resources() "mark_thread_resources()"

    @see @ref Qore::Thread::AbstractThreadResource "AbstractThreadResource" for an example of custom thread resource
    handling

    @section deadlocks Deadlocks

    %Qore supports deadlock detection in complex locking scenarios and will throw a \c THREAD-DEADLOCK exception
    rather than allow an operation to be performed that would cause a deadlock. Deadlock detection is implemented for
    internal locking (global variable and object access), @ref synchronized "synchronized" methods and functions, etc,
    as well as for all %Qore threading classes.

    %Qore can only detect deadlocks when a lock resource acquired by one thread is required by another who holds a
    lock that the first thread also needs. Other errors such as forgetting to unlock a global lock and trying to
    acquire that lock in another thread cannot be differentiated from valid use of threading primitives and will
    result in a process that never terminates (a deadlocked process). However, common threading errors such as trying
    to lock the same @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex" twice in the same thread without unlocking it between the two
    @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex::lock() "Mutex::lock()" calls are caught in %Qore and exceptions are thrown.
    Additionally, locks are tracked as thread resources, so if a thread terminates while holding a lock, an exception
    will be thrown and the lock will be automatically released.

    @section thread_cancellation Cooperative Thread Cancellation

    %Qore supports cooperative thread cancellation, which allows one thread to request that another thread terminate
    gracefully. Unlike forceful thread termination (which is unsafe and not supported), cooperative cancellation sets a
    flag that the target thread checks at well-defined @ref cancel_check_points "check points", raising a
    \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception when the flag is detected.

    @subsection cancel_api Cancellation API

    The thread cancellation API consists of three functions:

    |!Function|!Description
    |@ref Qore::cancel_thread() "cancel_thread(int tid, *string reason)"|Sets the cancellation flag on the target \
        thread identified by TID; an optional reason string is included in the \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception; returns \
        @ref True if the thread was found and the flag was set, @ref False if the thread has already exited
    |@ref Qore::thread_cancelled() "thread_cancelled()"|Returns @ref True if cancellation has been requested for the \
        current thread; this is a lightweight check (one atomic load) that does not raise an exception
    |@ref Qore::clear_thread_cancel() "clear_thread_cancel()"|Clears the cancellation flag for the current thread, \
        allowing the thread to continue running and pass through check points without triggering the exception again

    @ref Qore::cancel_thread() "cancel_thread()" and @ref Qore::clear_thread_cancel() "clear_thread_cancel()" require
    the \c THREAD_CONTROL functional domain. @ref Qore::thread_cancelled() "thread_cancelled()" only requires
    \c THREAD_INFO, so any code can poll its own cancellation status.

    @note Attempting to cancel the current thread raises a \c THREAD-CANCEL-ERROR exception. Cancelling an
    already-cancelled thread updates the reason string.

    @anchor cancel_check_points
    @subsection cancel_check_points_section Cancellation Check Points

    A cancellation check point is a location in program execution where the %Qore runtime checks the thread's
    cancellation flag. When cancellation is detected at a check point, a \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception is raised. The
    following operations are cancellation check points:

    - <b>Language-level loops:</b> \c while, \c for, \c foreach, and \c do-while check at the start of each iteration
    - <b>Sleep operations:</b> @ref Qore::sleep() "sleep()" and @ref Qore::usleep() "usleep()"
    - <b>Threading primitive waits:</b>
      - @ref Qore::Thread::Gate "Gate"::\c enter()
      - @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex"::\c lock()
      - @ref Qore::Thread::Counter "Counter"::\c waitForZero()
      - @ref Qore::Thread::Condition "Condition"::\c wait()
      - @ref Qore::Thread::RWLock "RWLock"::\c readLock() and \c writeLock()
    - <b>Auto-lock constructors:</b> @ref Qore::Thread::AutoLock "AutoLock",
      @ref Qore::Thread::AutoGate "AutoGate", @ref Qore::Thread::AutoReadLock "AutoReadLock", and
      @ref Qore::Thread::AutoWriteLock "AutoWriteLock"
    - <b>Queue operations:</b> @ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue"::\c get() and \c pop() (both timed and untimed)
    - <b>I/O operations:</b> pipe reads/writes, file locking, and backquote command execution

    Blocking check points (threading primitives, I/O) use a 500ms polling interval; the thread does not respond to
    cancellation instantly but will detect it within 500ms of the flag being set.

    @subsection cancel_handling Handling Cancellation

    When a \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception is raised, the thread can handle it like any other exception. The cancellation
    flag remains set after the exception is thrown, so any subsequent check point will raise the exception again unless
    the flag is cleared with @ref Qore::clear_thread_cancel() "clear_thread_cancel()".

    @par Example: Basic cancellation
    @code{.py}
%modern
Counter done();
int tid = background sub() {
    on_exit {
        done.dec();
    }
    done.inc();
    try {
        while (True) {
            do_work();  # cancellation checked at start of each iteration
        }
    } catch (hash<auto> ex) {
        if (ex.err == "THREAD-CANCELLED") {
            logger.info("cancelled: %s", ex.desc);
        }
    }
}();
sleep(5);
cancel_thread(tid, "timeout");
done.waitForZero();
    @endcode

    @par Example: Cleanup after cancellation
    @code{.py}
%modern
try {
    mutex.lock();
    on_exit {
        mutex.unlock();
    }
    process_data();
} catch (hash<auto> ex) {
    if (ex.err == "THREAD-CANCELLED") {
        # clear the flag so cleanup code can use check points without re-triggering
        clear_thread_cancel();
        save_partial_results();
        close_connections();
    }
}
    @endcode

    @subsection cancel_security Cancellation and Sandboxing

    Thread cancellation integrates with the @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" interrupt mechanism. Internally,
    both thread-level cancellation and program-level interrupt requests are checked by the same mechanism at check
    points. This means that when a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" is attached to a
    @ref Qore::Program "Program" and
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "SandboxManager::requestInterrupt()" is called, all threads
    executing in that program will receive a \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED exception at the next check point.

    Sandboxed code that does not have the \c THREAD_CONTROL functional domain cannot call
    @ref Qore::clear_thread_cancel() "clear_thread_cancel()", making cancellation effectively unavoidable. This is
    intentional: it ensures that untrusted code cannot ignore cancellation requests from the host environment.

    @since %Qore 2.3
*/
